// WHY: the Avra build system, lived inside @std/avrac because the
// build driver and the compiler ship together as one toolchain — same
// versioning, same release, same team. Modeled on cargo and
// go-build: fingerprint-keyed cache, dependency-graph-aware
// compilation, bottom-up parallel builds, progress UI. Used by any
// Avra project, including the bootstrap compiler itself.
//
// Layering:
//   * @std/avrac.parse / typeck / codegen / etc.   ← compile one file
//   * @std/avrac.build                              ← drive the build
//   * packages/cli (the bs2 binary)                 ← user surface
//
// Phases tracked under epic forge-crafting-intepreters-a1el:
//   Phase 1: fingerprint + cache the existing single-unit build
//   Phase 2: split @std/avrac as a separately-cached unit
//   Phase 3: per-module units, metadata files, parallel workers
//   Phase 4: progress bars, --timings, lockfile, profiles
//
// Public surface lives in sibling files; this file is the namespace
// root. Each sibling .av (cache.av, driver.av, fingerprint.av, …)
// auto-loads as part of the `build` module under g2eo.1
// directory-as-module semantics — no `mod` decls needed. Consumers
// reach machinery via:
//   use @std.avrac.build.fingerprint.{fp_for_unit, ...}
//
// ── START HERE: the facade (facade.av) ──────────────────────────────
//
// Most consumers want one of three calls, not the machinery below them.
// The facade owns every artifact — cache slots, the intermediate object,
// the linked binary — so a caller never names a path, runs llc, or
// reasons about freshness:
//
//     use @std.avrac.build.{build_request, build_executable}
//
//     mut req = build_request(project_dir)
//     req.entry = "src/app.av"          // "" → manifest [bin]/[lib]
//     let report = build_executable(req)
//     if !report.ok { eprintln(report.error) }
//
//   build_ir              source → cached LLVM IR
//   build_executable      source → native binary
//   link_unit_executable  the link tail for an already-built unit
//
// Three content-addressed caches — IR by unit fingerprint, object by IR
// content, binary by link inputs — make an unchanged rebuild a no-op.
//
// The facade NEVER exits the process: failures come back as
// `report.ok == false` with a printable `report.error`. Set
// `req.mode = BuildOutput.Quiet` when the build is a means to an end.
//
// Reach past the facade (decide_build / cache_* / link_objects) only for
// genuinely new orchestration. If you find yourself hand-rolling llc, a
// `.o` path, or a freshness stamp in a consumer, the facade is missing a
// function — add it THERE, not a second build path in the caller.
